INT. AIRPORT LONG-TERM PARKING STRUCTURE - LEVEL 4 - NIGHT

Concrete. Oil stains. Sodium lights hum. A lone car alarm somewhere, then silence.

VESTA KORHONEN (40s, charcoal coat, briefcase) walks to a sedan, keys out, shoulders tight. Footsteps echo behind her. She stops. They stop.

She turns. A man in a gray parka, ABEL TRUJILLO (50s, thick glasses), stands ten feet away, hands visible.

ABEL
Ms. Korhonen.

VESTA
I don't know you.

ABEL
You will.

VESTA
Back off.

ABEL
I'm not armed.

VESTA
Everybody says that.

ABEL
Check me.

VESTA
Absolutely not.

ABEL
Then listen. I worked with your brother.

VESTA
My brother worked alone.

ABEL
He told you that.

VESTA
He told me everything.

ABEL
He told you what he could.

VESTA
Where is he?

ABEL
Not here.

VESTA
Where?

ABEL
I can take you.

VESTA
Why would I go anywhere with you?

ABEL
Because you flew in for this. You wouldn't be here if you didn't half believe me.

VESTA
Don't tell me what I believe.

ABEL
Then tell me yourself.

VESTA
I believe my brother is dead.

ABEL
Is that what they told you?

VESTA
It's what the police told me.

ABEL
And what did you tell yourself?

She falls silent. Her grip on the briefcase whitens.

A third figure steps out of the dark behind Abel: SERGEANT LENNOX ODUYA (30s, patrol uniform, hand on holster).

LENNOX
Mr. Trujillo. Step away from the lady.

ABEL
Officer, there's no need --

LENNOX
Now.

Abel raises his hands. Vesta stares at Lennox, then at Abel, then at the ramp. Two more cars roll up from below, headlights blinding, and a woman and a man, HAYDEN PRYCE (30s) and ZELDA MORROW (40s), step out in dark suits.

HAYDEN
Ms. Korhonen? Federal agents. We need you to come with us.

VESTA
Why?

ZELDA
Because that man isn't who he says.

Abel meets her eyes, silent, then lowers his hands and turns his palms up: I told you.
